Historically, in Western societies, genealogy focused on the [[kinship and descent]] of rulers and nobles, often arguing or demonstrating the legitimacy of claims to wealth and power. Genealogy often overlapped with [[heraldry]], which reflected the ancestry of noble houses in their [[coats of arms]]. Modern scholars regard many claimed noble ancestries as fabrications, such as the [[Anglo-Saxon Chronicle]]'s tracing of the ancestry of several English kings to the god [[Woden]].<ref>Barbara Yorke,  "Anglo-Saxon origin legends." in ''Myth, Rulership, Church and Charters''  ed by Julia Barrow and Andrew Wareham. (Routledge, 2017) pp: 29–44.</ref> With the coming of Christianity to northern Europe, [[Anglo-Saxon royal genealogies]] extended the kings' lines of ancestry from Woden back to reach the line of [[Patriarchs (Bible)|Biblical patriarchs]]: [[Noah]]<ref>
